  "question": {
    "text": "A radioactive nucleus $n_2$ has 3 times the decay constant as compared to the decay constant of another radioactive nucleus $n_1$. If initial number of both nuclei are the same, what is the ratio of number of nuclei of $n_2$ to the number of nuclei of $n_1$, after one half-life of $n_1$ ?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "9pzea",
      "text": "$1 / 8$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "2mFQ6",
      "text": "$8$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "sdPp3",
      "text": "$4$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "xBpjk",
      "text": "$1 / 4$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned} & \\mathrm{N}_2=\\mathrm{N}_0 \\mathrm{e}^{-3 \\lambda t} \\\\ & \\mathrm{~N}_1=\\mathrm{N}_0 \\mathrm{e}^{-\\lambda \\mathrm{t}} \\\\ & \\frac{\\mathrm{N}_2}{\\mathrm{~N}_1}=\\mathrm{e}^{-2 \\lambda \\mathrm{t}} \\\\ & \\mathrm{t}_{\\text {half lifeofN }} \\mathrm{t} \\\\ & \\mathrm{t}=\\frac{\\ln 2}{\\lambda}{ }_{\\mathrm{n}} 1 \\\\ & \\frac{\\mathrm{~N}_0}{2}=\\mathrm{N}_0 \\mathrm{e}^{-\\lambda \\mathrm{t}}\\end{aligned}$\n\n\n$\\begin{aligned} & \\lambda \\mathrm{t}=\\ln 2 \\\\ & \\mathrm{t}=\\frac{\\ln 2}{\\lambda} \\\\ & =\\mathrm{e}^{-2 \\lambda \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\lambda}} \\\\ & \\frac{\\mathrm{~N}_2}{\\mathrm{~N}_1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
